===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|your's||archaic, now misspelling}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|youres}}, {{m|enm|ȝoures}}, attested since the 1300s. Equivalent to {{af|en|your|-s}} (compare {{m|en|-'s}}); formed by analogy to {{m|en|his}}. Displaced {{m|en|yourn}} in standard speech.<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/jɔːz/|/jʊəz/|/jəz/|q3=unstressed|a=RP,AU}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔː(ɹ)z|s=1}}
* {{enPR|yôrz|a=GA,CA}}, {{IPA|en|/joɹz/|/jʊɚz/|/jɝz/|/jɚz/|q4=unstressed}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-yours.ogg|a=US}}, {{audio|en|En-us-yours (alternate pronunciation).oga|a=US}}
* {{homophones|en|yaws|aa=in some <<non-rhotic>> accents}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔː(ɹ)z|s=1}}

===Pronoun===
{{head|en|pronoun|cat2=possessive pronouns|cat3=second person pronouns|plural|yours}}

# That or those belonging to [[you]]; {{n-g|the possessive second-person singular pronoun used without a following noun}}.
#: {{ux|en|If this edit is mine, the other must be '''yours'''.}}
#: {{ux|en| Their encyclopedia is good, but '''yours''' is even better.}}
#: {{ux|en| Are all these socks '''yours'''?}}
#: {{ux|en| The socks of '''yours''' are a bit niffy.|q=''[[double possessive]]''}}
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|IX|passage=“Heavens!” exclaimed Nina, “the blue-stocking and the fogy!—and '''yours''' ''are'' pale blue, Eileen!—you’re about as self-conscious as Drina—slumping there with your hair tumbling ''à la'' Mérode! Oh, it's very picturesque, of course, but a straight spine and good grooming is better.{{nb...}}”}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} Your house or home.
#: {{ux|en|Let's go over to '''yours'''.}}
# {{non-gloss|Written at the end of a letter, before the signature.}}
#: {{ux|en|[[yours sincerely|'''Yours''' sincerely]],&emsp; {{nowrap|[[yours faithfully|'''Yours''' faithfully]],}}&emsp; '''Yours''',&emsp; {{nowrap|Sincerely '''yours''',}}}}

====Usage notes====
* In British English the adverb almost invariably follows the word ''yours'' at the end of a letter; in most dialects of American English it usually precedes it. As a general rule, ''sincerely'' is only employed if the name of the recipient is already known to the writer; a letter begun with ''Dear Sir'' or ''Dear Madam'' finishes with ''faithfully''. ''Yours'' on its own and ''yours ever'' are less formal than the other forms.

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|yourn}} {{q|obsolete outside Britain and US dialects, especially Appalachia}}

